 ### Aokana
-#### definition
-- [S1] Streaming/LOD variant of DAG that splits the world into many shallow DAGs of 256³ Voxels each, with octree-style LOD levels (LOD n is 8 children merged at the same 256³ resolution) and distance-based streaming.
-#### pitfall
-- [S1] Game attributes cannot utilize complex lossy compression.
-#### number
-- [S1] On an RTX 3060 Ti it renders a 64K scene of about 10 billion Voxels in about 6 ms. Only about 2–17% of the data is resident: 424 MB VRAM against 23 GB on disk for San Miguel at 64K.
-- [S1] In Aokana's numbers, colour dominates storage: 21 GB of colour against 2.2 GB of geometry.
+Streaming/LOD variant of DAG that splits the world into many shallow DAGs of 256³ Voxels each, with octree-style LOD levels (LOD n is 8 children merged at the same 256³ resolution) and distance-based streaming [S1]. On an RTX 3060 Ti it renders a 64K scene of about 10 billion Voxels in about 6 ms. Only about 2–17% of the data is resident: 424 MB VRAM against 23 GB on disk for San Miguel at 64K [S1]. In Aokana's numbers, colour dominates storage: 21 GB of colour against 2.2 GB of geometry [S1]. Game attributes cannot utilize complex lossy compression [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The concept of using Directed Acyclic Graphs (DAGs) to compress voxel data originated from the need to reduce the memory overhead of Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVOs) in real-time rendering and visualization. The foundational work on bottom-up SVO to DAG reduction was introduced by Laine and Karras, who demonstrated that identical subtrees could be merged to form a DAG, significantly reducing memory usage for complex scenes [S1][S2]. This approach was later extended to pointerless representations (PSVDAG) to further optimize storage for streaming and archival purposes [S1]. The family has seen recent adaptations for real-time game engines, such as Aokana, which integrates DAG structures with Level of Detail (LOD) and streaming techniques to handle massive voxel datasets on consumer hardware [S1].
+The concept of Directed Acyclic Graphs in voxel representation emerged as a solution to the redundancy inherent in tree-based structures. While Octrees and Pointer Trees provide efficient sparsity encoding, they duplicate memory for identical geometric structures. The bottom-up reduction technique, which guarantees a minimal DAG, was developed to address this inefficiency [S1][S2]. Subsequent variants like PSVDAG and DAOG introduced alternative mechanisms for node sharing, such as pointerless binary encoding and hash-based isomorphism detection, to further optimize storage for streaming and archival purposes [S1, S2]. Recent applications, such as Aokana, have integrated DAGs into real-time rendering pipelines, demonstrating their viability for large-scale, high-resolution voxel scenes in interactive graphics [S1].
 
